
The InfoSec Store and Retrieve makes use of the Information Theoretic Secure Medley-One Cipher.
Information-theoretic security—such as the One-Cipher, which comes with a proof of security and hence is mathematically unbreakable as long as shared keys are as large as the messages themselves.

The Last Mile or Host<->Client link
The LinkInfo make use of the Public Domain SOne Cipher which comes with a proof of security.
This makes use of a multi-protocol but with default of ECDH-ES (X25519) asymmetric (public-key) cryptography with the following modifications:
1. The static X25519 keys are predeployed, hence the public key becomes a shared secret between the Host<>Client.
2. Should this public key become exposed:
a) The ECDH-ES shared key could become compromised.
b) in order to prevent this an out-of-channel 'Proof-of-Time' algorithm is used to compute a non deterministic 'shared secret'
This shared secret cannot be computed by any attacker due to the Proof-of-Time protocol.

The Information Object is retrieved via a SAS JwToken coupled with TLS protected download and finally Medley One Cipher protected One Link negotiated protocol.
The loss of access to a end device does not compromise retrieval of any stored information object.

The search space for Medley vectors is greater than 2 ^ 2048 or 10 ^ 616.
Cosmic Comparison:
Atoms in the Universe: The observable universe contains roughly 10⁸⁰ atoms.
Storage Reality: To store 2²⁵⁶ bytes, you would need to encode nearly one byte of data onto every single atom in the universe.
Note even if this Shannon max entropy were guessed, it would have zero effect on the Medley One Cipher security.

Proof of Time
The proof of time algorithm is a space-time domain solution, not just time domain.
In essence each link is bound to a single space time continuum amongst an infinite set of space-time continuums.
At the Point of the Key Distribution a single tick along this time continuum is selected from an infinite set of possible ticks.
This tick is deterministically computed at both the sender and receiver, and hence creates a single space-time context for the KD. 
